{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793692870,"nonce":"jL/4yFKHU4/wW4eDPV9trhWhuuU=","title":"Lua cold rows: a main chunk with loops is optimised and compiled before it runs","message":"Measured on the NUC (release, llvm-backend, taskset to 4 CPUs) with ZYNTAX_TRACE_LAZY=1 and ZYNTAX_TRACE_LOWER_PHASES=1. method_call.lua: wall 40 to 52 ms for 7.5 to 9.9 ms of timed work; the phases are runtime 0.2, compile 11.8, run 29.9 (shutdown 0.01 ms, so exit is not waiting for LLVM; zypy and zylua leave with flush then _exit, git-bug 0ec18dc). The run phase holds about 22 ms before the timed loop, and the trace shows why: lua$chunk is compiled on the warm-up thread in 22.5 ms, of which 17.8 ms is body preparation (the HIR optimisation passes) and 4.7 ms code generation, because the chunk has loops and takes the full optimiser path (a loop-free function takes the quick baseline in about 0.3 ms). nbody.lua: chunk 8.9 ms (body 6.9, compile 2.0); binary_trees.lua: 2.5 ms (body 0.0). The other 14 benchmarks print no lua$chunk compile line of this kind.\n\nOn a CI runner the same work takes about twice as long (method_call: wall 87 ms against 14.7 ms timed). LuaJIT starts its bytecode interpreter at once.\n\nNot a general cause: the 13 to 20 ms floor that every row carries is runtime start (hello world 6.7 ms on the NUC) plus lowering and first compiles. Direction for these two rows: start the chunk in the interpreter and let the optimised compile land through the resume points, or give a chunk whose loops are cold a cheaper pipeline.","files":null}]}